WHAT IS USED FROM IT
  ADAAG 307.2    protrusion limits, and the handrail exception
  ADAAG 307.4    vertical clearance over an accessible route
  ADAAG 304.3.1  circular turning space
  ADAAG 305.3    clear floor or ground space
  ADAAG 405.2    ramp running slope
  ADAAG 502.5    vertical clearance over a van route

  EVERY ADAAG DIMENSION IS PRINTED IN BOTH INCHES AND MILLIMETRES, AND EVERY
  PAIR IS A SOFT CONVERSION. 60 in is 1524 mm and the document prints 1525;
  30 in is 762 mm and it prints 760; 80 in is 2032 mm and it prints 2030; 4 in
  is 101.6 mm and it prints 100. Both figures are carried exactly as printed and
  nothing converts one into the other; tools/citecheck.py bans the six
  arithmetic products outright, because none of them appears in the document.

  That 762 is worth noticing on its own: it is the figure 29 CFR
  1910.303(g)(1)(i)(B) prints for the same 30 inches. Two documents, two
  published values for one length, and taking one for the other is exactly the
  confusion the audit exists to prevent.

  The handrail limit is printed as a vulgar fraction — "4 1/2 inches (115 mm)" —
  marked up in the source as 4<FR>1/2</FR>. The engine carries 4.5, and the
  audit checks the fraction in the clause and the decimal at the declaration
  site, requiring both.

  Table 405.2, which permits steeper ramp slopes in existing facilities, is NOT
  transcribed. 304.3.2, the T-shaped turning space, is NOT transcribed; only the
  circular form is reported.

  These are UNITED STATES requirements. CSA B651 governs accessible design in
  Canada and its dimensions differ; engine/mc-clearance.js names that gap rather
  than applying one country's figure under the other's label.

